      diveDeep: 'Recombinant DNA technology: (1) restriction endonucleases cut both human DNA and bacterial plasmid at the same recognition sequence, producing complementary "sticky ends"; (2) the human insulin gene is inserted into the plasmid; (3) DNA ligase seals the bonds; (4) the recombinant plasmid is inserted into E. coli bacteria; (5) bacteria replicate and produce insulin using the human gene. Before this technology, diabetics needed insulin from pig or cow pancreases, which sometimes caused immune reactions. Human-derived recombinant insulin is safer and more compatible. This is one of the most tested biotechnology topics on the Living Environment Regents.',
      modelAnswer: 'Genetic engineering is used to produce human insulin as follows: First, scientists isolate the gene for human insulin from human DNA using restriction enzymes, which cut DNA at specific base sequences. The same restriction enzymes are used to cut open a bacterial plasmid (a small circular piece of DNA), producing sticky ends on both the gene and the plasmid. The human insulin gene is then inserted into the plasmid, and DNA ligase is used to seal the bonds, creating recombinant DNA. This recombinant plasmid is introduced into bacterial cells. As the bacteria reproduce, they carry and express the human insulin gene, producing human insulin. This technique is useful because bacteria can be grown in large quantities very quickly, producing large amounts of human insulin that is compatible with the human immune system. Before this technology, insulin had to be harvested from pigs and cows, which sometimes caused immune reactions in diabetic patients.',
