      diveDeep: 'Carrying capacity (K) is the maximum population size an environment can sustainably support, given available food, water, shelter, and space. Population growth follows a logistic (S-shaped) curve: exponential growth at first when resources are abundant, then slowing as the population approaches K, eventually leveling off. If the population temporarily exceeds K, density-dependent limiting factors (food shortage, disease, predation) cause it to crash back down. Density-dependent factors (food, water, disease, predation, competition) are more important as population density increases. Density-independent factors (weather, natural disasters) can limit population at any size.',
      modelAnswer: 'As the deer population approaches the carrying capacity of 500, growth rate will slow because resources become increasingly scarce. As more deer compete for the same limited food and water, birth rates will decrease and death rates will increase. If the population temporarily exceeds 500, the limiting factors will cause the population to decline back toward the carrying capacity. Two factors that would limit deer population growth are: (1) Food availability — as deer numbers increase, competition for plant material intensifies; deer that cannot obtain enough food will be less healthy, reproduce less, and have higher mortality; (2) Predation — as deer become more numerous, predator populations (such as wolves or mountain lions) may grow, increasing predation pressure on the deer. Together, these density-dependent factors prevent unlimited population growth and keep the deer population near the carrying capacity.',
