      modelAnswer: 'Mitosis and meiosis are both forms of cell division but differ in important ways:\n\nPurpose: Mitosis is used for growth, development, and repair of body tissues. When you heal a cut, mitosis produces new skin cells. Meiosis is used to produce gametes (sex cells — sperm and eggs) for sexual reproduction.\n\nCells produced: Mitosis produces two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell (diploid, 2n = 46 chromosomes in humans). Meiosis produces four daughter cells that are genetically different from each other and from the parent cell (haploid, n = 23 chromosomes in humans).\n\nGenetic variation: Mitosis produces no genetic variation — all daughter cells are clones of the parent. Meiosis introduces genetic variation through two mechanisms: crossing-over (exchange of DNA segments between homologous chromosomes during meiosis I) and independent assortment (random alignment of chromosome pairs). This variation is the raw material for natural selection and evolution.\n\nContribution to life: Mitosis allows a single fertilized egg (zygote) to develop into a complete organism with trillions of cells, all carrying the same genetic information. Meiosis ensures that when gametes fuse during fertilization, the chromosome number is maintained (23 + 23 = 46) and genetic diversity is generated, promoting the survival of the species.',
